    "q-223598": {
      "dom_id": "step-q223598",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14164726/assets/e874f46cd9a95c8bff5316128c72f4fd216194e44f54dc0929f6ad53a2e6a4c9.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<\\,f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $-4.$ This occurs at the point $(-4,-4).$ However note that this point is not actually included in the graph.\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $5.$ This occurs at the point $(4,5).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-4$ and $5,$ including $5$ but not including $-4.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-4<y\\le 5,$ which can also be written as $-4<f(x)\\le 5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
